Your mental model is spot-on: data travels over the network as a lightweight JSON string, gets stored in MongoDB as BSON (Binary JSON), and is parsed into a pure JavaScript object on both the backend and frontend.

How the Data Transforms at Each Layer


Layer	Data Format	Why It Uses This Format
1. Network (HTTP Transit)	JSON String (plain text)	Lightweight, human-readable, and language-independent (JSON.stringify()).
2. Backend (Node / Express)	JavaScript Object	Middleware like express.json() parses incoming text so your code can read req.body.email.
3. Database (MongoDB)	BSON (Binary JSON)	Faster to scan and parse than text, and supports extra data types like ObjectId and Date that plain JSON lacks.
4. Frontend (Browser / React)	JavaScript Object	res.json() or Axios converts the response string back into a JS object so you can render user.fullName in your UI.


Two Subtle Details to Keep in Mind

• JSON is text, not a JS object. While JSON syntax looks identical to a JavaScript object literal, data moving across fetch() or HTTP is always a serialized string until parsed.
• BSON vs. JS objects. Inside your Node.js code, Mongoose converts MongoDB's BSON documents into JavaScript objects (or Mongoose Documents). When you send them back with res.json(user), special BSON types like ObjectId and Date automatically serialize into standard strings in the JSON response.
