Yes, you nailed every single point—and that .json() naming confusion is one of the most common misconceptions in web development.

1. What Does "Parse" Mean?

Parsing means reading raw, unorganized text (a string) and translating it into a structured format that your code can actually interact with.
• Before parsing (String): '{"fullName":"Ayesha Khan"}' — JavaScript sees this as one flat piece of text. If you try data.fullName, you get undefined.
• After parsing (JS Object): { fullName: "Ayesha Khan" } — JavaScript now understands keys and values, so data.fullName gives you "Ayesha Khan".
• The opposite of parsing is Stringifying: JSON.stringify(obj) takes a live JS object and flattens it back into a plain text string so it can travel over the internet.

2. MongoDB Stores BSON, Not Pure JS Objects

JavaScript objects only exist inside your computer's temporary memory (RAM) while Node.js is running. You cannot save a live JS memory object directly onto a hard drive.
• When Mongoose saves your JS object, the MongoDB driver translates it into BSON (Binary JSON)—a compact binary format written to disk.
• BSON looks like JSON conceptually, but it is stored in binary bytes (0s and 1s) and supports richer types like ObjectId, Date, and Decimal128 that standard JSON strings do not have.
• When you fetch data back with User.find(), the driver reads the BSON from disk and reconstructs a JS object in RAM for your backend to use.

3. The .json() Misconception (Frontend vs. Backend)

You caught a real naming trap! The word .json() actually does two opposite things depending on whether you are receiving data on the frontend or sending data on the backend:

Where You Use It	What It Actually Does	Under the Hood
Frontend fetch()
const data = await response.json();	JSON String → JS Object
It reads the incoming response body stream and parses the JSON text into a usable JS object.	Equivalent to JSON.parse(responseBodyText)
Backend Express
res.json(userObject);	JS Object → JSON String
It takes your JS object, converts it into a JSON text string, and sends it over HTTP to the frontend.	Equivalent to JSON.stringify(userObject)

So on the frontend, response.json() does not "convert into JSON"—the data arriving from the server is already JSON text. Calling .json() tells the browser: "Read this incoming JSON body and parse it into a JavaScript object for me."
