Almost completely right—the only tiny tweak is the word "parse" on the backend: on the frontend response.json() parses JSON text into a JS object, while on the backend res.json() serializes (stringifies) a JS object into JSON text to send.
Here is the exact distinction so the terminology is 100% locked in:
• Frontend (await response.json()): Parses incoming JSON text into a structured JS object (JSON text ──▶ JS object).
• Backend (res.json(data)): Serializes (stringifies) your JS object into a lightweight JSON string and sends it over HTTP (JS object ──▶ JSON text). (And on the backend, it is app.use(express.json()) that does the parsing of incoming request bodies into req.body.)

Quick Cheat Sheet


Direction	Method	Input	Output	Technical Term
Frontend → Backend	JSON.stringify(form)	JS Object	JSON String	Serialization
Backend Receiving	app.use(express.json())	JSON String	JS Object (req.body)	Parsing
Backend → Frontend	res.json(user)	JS Object	JSON String	Serialization
Frontend Receiving	await res.json()	JSON String	JS Object	Parsing

You have the entire data flow clear now: strings travel across the wire, objects live in your code, and BSON lives on MongoDB's disk.
